Use CueRelativePosition to extract a subtitle block in one read

Real remuxes record, for every cue point, the byte offset of the referenced block inside its
cluster: the 8.2 GB remux in the benchmark has it on 1873 of 1873 cue points, written by both
ffmpeg and mkvmerge. The reader ignored it and walked the cluster's blocks instead - a remux
cluster holds ~150 of them, mostly audio frames - so an episode with 344 subtitle cue points
cost tens of thousands of small reads. That is why extraction was still slow on a real library
after the earlier index work, while looking fine on synthetic files.

Measured on the same files with identical output: real 8.2 GB remux 127 reads / 7.0 MB ->
28 reads / 0.1 MB (27 ms), and a 1,400-subtitle fixture 1,421 reads / 91.8 MB -> 1,413 reads /
5.8 MB. The output is byte-identical to ffmpeg's own extraction of the same track. Clusters
that lack the offset, or whose shape does not match, still take the walking path.

Also fixes a time offset the indexed path introduced: CueTime is the seek point's timestamp,
which for the referenced block is already its own time, so adding the block's relative
timecode double-counted it (+51 ms / +459 ms against ffmpeg). The cluster Timecode element is
the base now, as in the walking path.

Extraction progress reports its cost while it runs (reading subtitle 80/344 (1.2 MB, 96
reads)) so a slow read is distinguishable from a slow disk.

Harness: 85 checks, ALL PASS, including that the indexed path reads less than the walk on
identical fixtures and that block offsets are actually used.

Version 1.1.0.25.
